# Production Pipeline for High-Realism 3D Talking Avatars (Web Browser)

## Goal

Generate a production-ready real-time talking avatar from either:

* A single photo (minimum input)
* A short selfie video (preferred input)

The final avatar must support:

* Real-time lip sync
* Real-time facial expressions
* Head pose animation
* Browser rendering (WebGL/WebGPU)
* ARKit-compatible blendshapes
* Future migration to Gaussian Avatars

---

# 1. Input Acquisition

## Option A — Single Photo

Accept:

* Frontal face image
* Minimum 1024×1024
* Neutral expression preferred

## Option B — Selfie Video (Preferred)

Accept:

* 5–15 second video
* User slowly rotates head:

  * Left profile
  * Left 45°
  * Frontal
  * Right 45°
  * Right profile
  * Slightly up
  * Slightly down

Video should always be preferred when available because it provides significantly better identity reconstruction than a single image.

---

# 2. Multi-View Generation (Photo Input Only)

If the user uploads only a single photo:

Generate synthetic views to improve reconstruction.

## Recommended Approach

Use OpenAI Images 2 as primary generator.

Fallback:

* nanoBanana

Generate approximately 9–12 consistent views:

### Horizontal Angles

* -90°
* -60°
* -30°
* 0°
* +30°
* +60°
* +90°

### Vertical Angles

* Up
* Neutral
* Down

Important:

The generated views must preserve:

* Identity
* Hairline
* Ears
* Nose shape
* Eye spacing
* Facial proportions

Do not use generated views directly for rendering.

Generated views are reconstruction aids only.

---

# 2.1 User Processing Experience

While reconstruction is running:

Display:

* Original uploaded image
* Generated multiview images

Animate images in a clockwise orbit converging toward the frontal image.

Purpose:

* Communicate that multiple viewpoints are being analyzed
* Increase perceived processing quality
* Improve user trust in avatar generation

This animation is purely visual and does not affect reconstruction.

---

# 3. Keyframe Extraction (Video Input Only)

If input is video:

Extract representative frames:

* Left profile
* Left 45°
* Frontal
* Right 45°
* Right profile
* Upward view
* Downward view

Use these frames instead of synthetic views.

---

# 4. Identity Reconstruction

Primary model:

* MICA

Repository:

[https://github.com/Zielon/MICA](https://github.com/Zielon/MICA)

Output:

* FLAME identity parameters
* Stable facial geometry
* Consistent face shape

This becomes the canonical identity representation.

---

# 5. Detailed Facial Reconstruction

Use:

* EMOCA (preferred)

Fallback:

* DECA

Repositories:

[https://github.com/radekd91/emoca](https://github.com/radekd91/emoca)

[https://github.com/yfeng95/DECA](https://github.com/yfeng95/DECA)

Generate:

* Facial geometry
* Expression space
* Eyelids
* Lips
* Nose details
* Skin detail maps

Output:

High-quality FLAME-compatible face mesh.

---

# 6. Avatar Completion

FLAME alone is insufficient for production avatars.

Add missing components.

## Eyes

Generate:

* Eyeballs
* Iris
* Pupil
* Eye shaders

Eyes must be explicit geometry.

Do not rely on texture-only eyes.

---

## Teeth

Generate:

* Upper teeth
* Lower teeth

Use procedural templates.

Required for realistic speech animation.

---

## Neck

Generate complete neck geometry.

---

## Shoulders and Upper Torso

Use:

* SMPL-X

Repository:

[https://smpl-x.is.tue.mpg.de/](https://smpl-x.is.tue.mpg.de/)

Generate:

* Neck
* Shoulders
* Upper chest

The final avatar should not be floating-head only.

---

# 7. Rig Generation

Convert reconstructed avatar into a production rig.

Generate:

## FLAME Rig

For internal processing.

## ARKit Blendshapes

Generate full ARKit-compatible blendshape set.

Examples:

* jawOpen
* eyeBlinkLeft
* eyeBlinkRight
* mouthSmileLeft
* mouthSmileRight
* browInnerUp

Target:

52+ ARKit blendshapes.

This becomes the universal animation interface.

---

# 8. Export Formats

Generate:

* GLB
* GLTF
* FLAME parameters

Store:

* Mesh
* Textures
* Blendshapes
* Rig data

---

# 9. Gaussian Avatar Generation


Convert mesh avatar into Gaussian representation.

Candidate projects:

* GaussianAvatars
* InstantSplat

Outputs:

* Gaussian point representation
* Realistic facial rendering
* Improved visual fidelity

This stage is optional and should not block avatar creation.

---

# 10. Talking Head Animation

Use:

## Primary

LivePortrait

[https://github.com/KwaiVGI/LivePortrait](https://github.com/KwaiVGI/LivePortrait)

## Alternatives

MuseTalk

[https://github.com/TMElyralab/MuseTalk](https://github.com/TMElyralab/MuseTalk)

Hallo2

[https://github.com/fudan-generative-vision/hallo2](https://github.com/fudan-generative-vision/hallo2)

Inputs:

* Audio
* TTS output
* Live microphone

Outputs:

* Facial expressions
* Lip synchronization
* Head motion
* Eye motion

Drive ARKit blendshapes generated in Stage 7.

---

# 11. Browser Runtime

Target:

* WebGL
* WebGPU

Recommended stack:

* Three.js
* React Three Fiber

Capabilities:

* Real-time rendering
* Blendshape animation
* Lip sync
* Expression control
* Camera control

The browser runtime should consume:

```text
GLB Avatar
+
ARKit Blendshapes
+
Animation Stream
```

and render the avatar entirely client-side.

---

